From: rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm6375: real big.LITTLE topology + CPU capacities

SM6375 is 2x Cortex-A78 (cpu6-7, up to 2208 MHz) + 6x Cortex-A55 (cpu0-5, up to
1804.8 MHz), but mainline's sm6375.dtsi describes all eight cores in a single
cpu-map cluster0 and gives none of them a capacity-dmips-mhz. The scheduler
therefore sees a flat, symmetric 8-way SMP machine: it cannot tell the two fast
cores from the six slow ones, so a latency-sensitive UI thread (the compositor,
the render thread) can be placed on a 1.8 GHz A55 while the A78s sit idle, and
CONFIG_ENERGY_MODEL / EAS stays inert because EAS only engages on an
asymmetric-capacity topology.

Two changes fix that, both pure device tree:

  - capacity-dmips-mhz on every CPU: 403 for the A55 little cores, 1024 for the
    A78 big cores. These are the standard ARM per-MHz DMIPS ratings; the kernel
    multiplies each by the core's max OPP and normalises, so the A78s come out at
    1024 and the A55s well below, which is what makes them "big" and "little" to
    the scheduler.
  - split the cpu-map into cluster0 (the six A55) and cluster1 (the two A78), so
    the topology matches the hardware.

With both, asymmetric-capacity scheduling and EAS activate and the scheduler
steers interactive threads onto the A78 cores. This is the largest non-GPU
fluidity win on the device (the GPU is already at its 840 MHz fuse ceiling).

Values cross-checked against mainline dts that already carry capacities
(talos.dtsi A55=1024/A76=1740 style; sm8150 uses 488/1024) and the ARM DMIPS/MHz
figures for A55 vs A78.

Measured on the device after flashing (kali-boot-rhodep-clean-20260903.img):

	/sys/.../cpu0/cpu_capacity   329     (A55)
	/sys/.../cpu6/cpu_capacity   1024    (A78)
	cpu0 topology/cluster_id     0
	cpu6 topology/cluster_id     1
	/sys/kernel/debug/energy_model/   present  -> EAS engaged

So the scheduler now sees the asymmetry (329 vs 1024) and an Energy Model is
registered, which is the definitive sign EAS is active rather than inert. Note
the A55 lands at 329 not 403: the kernel scales capacity-dmips-mhz by each
cluster's max OPP and normalises the biggest to 1024, so 403*1804.8MHz vs
1024*2208MHz gives ~329 for the little cores -- exactly the intended big/little
split.
---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
index ec7e87b..9cd56dd 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
@@ -48,6 +48,7 @@
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_0>;
 			qcom,freq-domain = <&cpufreq_hw 0>;
 			operating-points-v2 = <&cpu0_opp_table>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <403>;
 			interconnects = <&cpucp_l3 MASTER_EPSS_L3_APPS &cpucp_l3 SLAVE_EPSS_L3_SHARED>;
 			power-domains = <&cpu_pd0>;
 			power-domain-names = "psci";
@@ -74,6 +75,7 @@
 			next-level-cache = <&l2_100>;
 			qcom,freq-domain = <&cpufreq_hw 0>;
 			operating-points-v2 = <&cpu0_opp_table>;
